**STATUS T29:** [x] DONE 2026-10-23 (the second agent built part 1, the third agent part 2 and the tests), applied to the real project - IN THE FRIENDS' BUILDS AND THE EDITOR ONLY (`ETR_DEV_TOOLS`, which `DemoBuild` turns on; a release build gets stubs) and THE DEVELOPER LIST IS EMPTY until the owner adds his Steam ID (`Assets\Resources\DevAccounts.txt`, one ID per line, `#` comments). HOW TO USE IT: (1) open the terminal (CTRL + F1), type `@Catdog813` and Enter, then `steamid`: it prints your Steam ID and says whether it is on the list; put the ID on a line of `DevAccounts.txt` - the HOST reads the list, so the build the host plays has to carry it; (2) the host makes a lobby (its 5-character code is on its lobby page); (3) in your build: CTRL + F1, `@Catdog813`, `spectate CODE`, close the terminal (CTRL + F1): you are in the lobby as an invisible spectator (a page that says so); (4) when the host presses START RUN the camera follows player 1; A / D or the mouse buttons go to the next / the one before, F = a free camera (W A S D, E up, Q down, Shift fast, Ctrl slow), O = the overlay (over every guard: what he does, how far his radio call is, whom he is after, his health, ARMED / BACKUP / BOSS; over every player: his number and PINNED / BUSTED), N = the network panel (round trip, packet loss, bytes per second, tick rate, frame rate, players, how old the host's last record is), F1 = the spectator's dev menu (cash, go to a level, complete the quota, stun the guards, call or cancel the backup, god mode, free the team): every command goes to the host, which runs it. WHAT IT IS: the spectator never joins the Steam lobby (so no seat, no name in the party list and no message to anybody: fully invisible), connects straight to the host whose Steam ID the lobby names (`NetConst.KeyHost`, a lobby that is full is found too), and the host lets him in only if Steam itself says the account is on the list (`SteamNetworkTransport.TryGetRemoteSteamId`; the Editor's local transport has no Steam, so there a test's claim is taken), the build has the tools, the run has NOT started (before Start only, as the owner said), fewer than two watch and the version matches; he gets no body, no vote in the intro, no place in the party size or the five-player limit (`DevAccessRules`, `NetSession.PlayersConnected`), his microphone is never open and he hears nobody; commands from anybody else are dropped and are rate limited (`RunSync.Dev.cs`, `DevCommandRules`). Part 2: the free camera, the overlay and the network panel (`DevSpectator`, `DevSpectatorView`, `DevOverlayRules`), with the health and the target of every guard sent by the host to the spectators only, about four times a second (`RunSync.DevView.cs`). CHECKED: the pure checks (`Asks1023HTest` and `Asks1023ITest`) and on two machines over the Editor's loopback `DevSpectate` (host 10 and spectator 19 checks, no exception on either: he joins the lobby and is no player, the run starts and expects one player, the camera follows player 1, the dev menu opens, cash and a level arrive and bad commands and a command from a non-spectator are refused, the host's record has the wounded guard's health and the chaser's target, F flies the camera 7 m a second and gives it back, the network panel has its numbers) and `DevDenied` (2 and 2 checks: an account that is not on the list is refused with the reason, a developer after Start too). NOT checked: over STEAM (the code search that finds a full lobby, the host's Steam ID in the lobby data, the account proof - all need a second Steam account), a spectator who stays through several runs, a join into a run already under way (not built, as agreed), the keys with a real keyboard, the packet loss number (Steam only), and how the overlay and the panel look (no pictures).
